WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.140
Disney's live-action Aladdin dives much deeper
into Jasmine's backstory revealing the dark

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.870
history of the young princess's mother and
some hidden clues as to who was responsible

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.660
for her untimely death.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.440
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing the secret truth

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.930
about Jasmine's mum and Jafar's nefarious
connection to her past.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:24.520
There will be discussing a few spoilers for
Jasmine and Jafar's backstory, so just be

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.870
aware if you haven't seen the film yet.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.670
And don't forget my Aladdin giveaway is also
running, so be sure to subscribe and leave

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.910
a comment for your chance to win.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.910
The live-action version of Aladdin modifies
a number of things about the story in Disney's

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.390
original animation.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.440
The crucial change for what we're discussing
here is the conversation between Jasmine and

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.640
Aladdin when they first meet.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.760
Aladdin says the princess "should get out
[of the palace] more as the people haven't

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.880
seen her in years".

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.149
Jasmine, who's in disguise at this moment,
tells Aladdin that the princess isn't allowed

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:57.380
to go outside because "ever since the queen
was killed [...] the Sultan's been afraid

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.970
and [Jasmine's] been kept locked away."

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.930
Aladdin replies that since then it feels like
everyone in Agrabah has been afraid and that

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.630
the people of the city didn't have anything
to do with the queen's death.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.729
In fact, he mentions that she was particularly
loved by everyone.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.119
Now, it sounds like the death of Jasmine's
mother caused quite a stir and something about

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:20.619
it made the city's population fearful, meaning
it very likely wasn't an accident, but rather

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:23.939
people suspected it of being an assassination.

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:27.469
Jasmine's mother was originally from the kingdom
of Shehrabad, which in the world of Aladdin

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:32.180
is located in South Asia, and her wedding
to the Sultan of Agrabah was an arranged marriage

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.310
that united the two countries to form an alliance.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.929
Princess Jasmine's dual heritage means she's
half South Asian and half Arab and it's why

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.971
her outfits in the new movie are inspired
by South Asian clothing designs from her mother's

00:01:43.971 --> 00:01:45.649
side of the family.

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:49.599
Against this backdrop, in the new movie, we
also get a scene where Jafar is arguing with

00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:54.069
the Sultan that Agrabah should be forming
a military alliance with Prince Anders of

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:55.069
Skanland.

00:01:55.069 --> 00:01:59.340
Jafar claims that Agrabah's enemies are amassing
against them and he's annoyed when Jasmine

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.509
turns down the potential marriage alliance
with Prince Anders who by the way brought

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:07.719
cannons with him to show off to Jasmine in
a scene that was cut from the movie.

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:12.790
The villainous Royal Vizier is keen to start
a war and he specifically mentions Shehrabad,

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.050
Jasmine's mother's homeland, as a target.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.410
When the Sultan tells Jafar that Shehrabad
is Agrabah's oldest ally and that they shouldn't

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.040
be dragged into war with them, Jafar replies
that they used to be their ally.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.830
It sounds like something happened that broke
the close bond between Agrabah and Shehrabad,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:30.750
and that event is very likely the suspicious
nature of Jasmine's mother's death.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.780
Before she became queen of Agrabah, she was
probably a princess or other royalty in her

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.840
homeland and so her murder would have sent
shockwaves in that part of the world.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.380
In the live-action film, Jafar's backstory
is also built up a lot more than in the original

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.540
animation.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.790
And in a scene set in the dungeons, he gives
a classic villain monologue explaining what

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.240
he's done to get to where he is today.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.950
Just before he dispatches one of his minions
down a well, Jafar reveals that he spent five

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.700
years in a Shehrabad jail and he also talks
about "all the bodies he's buried."

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.410
So, let's connect all of these details together.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.610
First of all, Jasmine's mother died in mysterious,
suspicious circumstances.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:14.060
Then we've got the fact that Jafar was previously
imprisoned in Shehrabad, the South Asian kingdom

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.680
where Jasmine's mother is from.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.170
And he's now scheming to convince the Sultan
that Agrabah should be preparing for a war

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.180
with Shehrabad.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.190
I'd say that it's very likely that Jafar plotted
and carried out the murder of Jasmine's mother,

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.600
and that he did that for two big reasons.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.319
By getting rid of the Sultan's wife, it made
Jafar's rise to power much easier as it would

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:37.700
be simpler for him to get closer to the king
and influence him, either just with his cunning

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.590
plans or with his hypnotic snake staff.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.440
On top of that, Jafar seems hell-bent on trying
to start a war with Shehrabad and an effective

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.130
way to provoke their people into that would
be by killing their princess in suspicious

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:51.770
circumstances.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.660
Jafar's personal reasons for wanting war are
twofold.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.140
First, he's probably itching to get revenge
on the kingdom for incarcerating him for five

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.140
years.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.610
We don't know exactly what he was doing in
Shehrabad that got him locked up, but he did

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:07.380
reveal to Aladdin that he'd been a thief so
perhaps it had something to do with how the

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.319
evil sorcerer got his magical snake staff.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:14.770
In addition, starting such a war might create
the necessary chaos that would enable Jafar

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:19.590
to overthrow the Sultan completely and take
charge himself, for example if the Sultan

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.889
died in the war or if the war was too unpopular
with the people of Agrabah.

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:27.969
Maybe he's like Littlefinger from Game of
Thrones, and thinks he can end up on top by

00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:30.539
climbing 'the ladder of chaos'.

00:04:30.539 --> 00:04:34.810
And there's actually an intriguing hint as
to what Jafar might have done to Jasmine's

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.710
mother in the original animation in the single
scene where she was mentioned:

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.449
"I don't know where she gets it from.

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:44.539
Her mother wasn't nearly so picky.

00:04:44.539 --> 00:04:45.849
Oh!

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:48.479
Ah, Jafar.

00:04:48.479 --> 00:04:50.810
My most trusted advisor."

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:56.189
Notice how just after the Sultan talks about
Jasmine's mother, Jafar arrives and his menacing

00:04:56.189 --> 00:04:58.680
shadow falls over the Sultan.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.430
Knowing what we now know from the new movie,
I think we can read a lot more into the original

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.050
scene from the animation.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.229
Now, there is also another interesting maternal
connection that the new movie introduces in

00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:12.639
a little moment where Jasmine picks up an
oud and starts playing a tune.

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:16.379
Aladdin recognises it immediately and tells
Jasmine that his mother taught him that song

00:05:16.379 --> 00:05:18.729
and Jasmine says her mother did too.

00:05:18.729 --> 00:05:22.889
Some fans have speculated this means that
Aladdin and Jasmine's mothers are one and

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:25.349
the same, making Jasmine and Aladdin siblings.

00:05:25.349 --> 00:05:29.570
Curiously, there is that funny scene in the
animated movie where Jasmine has to pretend

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:33.599
to be Aladdin's sister after she's accosted
for taking an apple from a merchant's cart

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.779
to give to/for a hungry child.

00:05:34.779 --> 00:05:37.409
"You, uh, know this girl?

00:05:37.409 --> 00:05:38.460
Sadly, yes.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.770
She is my sister.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:41.770
She's a little crazy."

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:43.559
"No harm done.

00:05:43.559 --> 00:05:45.330
Now, come along, sis.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.349
Time to go see the doctor.

00:05:46.349 --> 00:05:47.580
Oh, hello, Doctor.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.810
How are you?"

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:53.409
One possible explanation for the shared mother
theory is that Jasmine's mum had an affair

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:57.460
or gave birth to Aladdin before she married
the Sultan which would make Jasmine and Aladdin

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:58.810
half-sister and brother.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:03.309
However, I think it's very unlikely Disney
would imply this, as it would make Aladdin

00:06:03.309 --> 00:06:09.159
and Jasmine's relationship incestuous going
beyond Luke and Leia's kiss in Star Wars and

00:06:09.159 --> 00:06:11.139
more full-on Game of Thrones.

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:15.550
The most likely explanation is that the song
Jasmine plays for Aladdin is quite simply

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.409
a popular one and so was known by both their
mothers.

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:22.940
Another possibility is that the song is from
Shehrabad and that both their mothers have

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.050
some connection to that country.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:29.900
In fact, the real meaning behind this moment
is to show an additional emotional connection

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.120
between Jasmine and Aladdin, on top of the
way in both movies each of them says they

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.120
feel trapped.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.719
"You're always scraping for food and ducking
the guards."

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:40.430
"You're not free to make your own choices."

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:41.800
"Sometimes you feel so..."

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.360
"You're just...
...trapped."

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.990
For Jasmine, her character arc in the new
movie is much more about taking forward her

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.460
mother's legacy and helping the people of
Agrabah.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:55.589
At one point, Jasmine mentions that her mother
would be unhappy at the state of Agrabah today

00:06:55.589 --> 00:06:59.949
which is why Jasmine knows she would be the
best ruler for the kingdom.

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:03.869
Originally in the animated movie, Aladdin
also shared a strong emotional connection

00:07:03.869 --> 00:07:05.020
to his mother.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:09.839
That was deleted from the 1992 film, but was
developed further in Disney's Broadway version

00:07:09.839 --> 00:07:13.990
of the story, in particular with the song
"Proud of Your Boy" which was initially written

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.710
for but ended up being scrapped from the animated
movie.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:22.080
And to learn more about all of the other changes
in the new live-action film, tap here to watch

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.069
my full video on that and there's also a link
in the video description.

00:07:26.069 --> 00:07:29.979
Over the years, Disney has been criticised
for its habit of killing off the mothers of

00:07:29.979 --> 00:07:34.870
many of its characters, and in Aladdin, it's
not just Jasmine whose mother is dead, but

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:36.749
Aladdin is also an orphan.

00:07:36.749 --> 00:07:40.759
One theory is that after Walt Disney felt
responsible for the death of his own mother,

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:45.210
the studio informally adopted a kind of "no
mothers" policy in many of the stories they

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:46.210
developed.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.210
"Do you have daddy issues?"

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.690
"I don't even have a mom."

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.129
"Neither do we!"

00:07:50.129 --> 00:07:54.759
More recently Ron Clements, co-director of
the 1992 Aladdin, said that the main reason

00:07:54.759 --> 00:07:58.869
for dead parents in many of Disney's stories
is that the original fairy tales on which

00:07:58.869 --> 00:08:01.289
the films are based also have missing parents.

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:05.089
And it seems that Disney may be starting to
address this issue.

00:08:05.089 --> 00:08:09.309
The live-action remakes seem to have gone
out of their way to introduce new backstories

00:08:09.309 --> 00:08:11.389
for the protagonists' missing mothers.

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:15.539
Beauty and the Beast added a new song for
the Beast called "Days In The Sun" in which

00:08:15.539 --> 00:08:18.460
we learn that the prince's mother had died
from an illness.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:23.240
Likewise, Belle's mum is revealed to have
died from the plague soon after her daughter's

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.800
birth, and there's a new song about her called
"How Does A Moment Last Forever".

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.969
And in Cinderella, we learned that Ella's
mother also died from an illness and it was

00:08:31.969 --> 00:08:36.910
on her deathbed that she asked her daughter
to promise to have courage and be kind.

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:41.350
In addition to that, Although Elsa and Anna's
parents are [mainly] absent in Frozen and

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:45.690
Vanellope from Wreck-It Ralph admits she doesn't
have a mum either, some of Disney's recent

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:50.000
animations such as Moana and Tangled do have
mothers for their protagonists.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.740
So, do you like the changes Disney made to
Jasmine and Jafar in the new Aladdin movie?

